LESSONS.push(
{id:"C12", g:"cmp", heat:3, title:"Internet & the World Wide Web", hi:"इंटरनेट एवं वर्ल्ड वाइड वेब", blocks:[
  {k:"hot", h:"Almost every paper has one internet question: <b>who invented the WWW</b>, the <b>parts of a URL</b>, what <b>DNS</b> does, a <b>browser vs search engine</b> swap, or a TLD like <b>.gov / .edu</b>."},
  {k:"story", h:"<p>Think of the <b>internet</b> as the road network of the whole world: millions of computers joined by cables, towers and satellites. The <b>World Wide Web</b> is only one kind of traffic on those roads, just as buses are only one kind of vehicle. Email, video calls and file transfer also travel on the same internet but are not the web.</p><p>The web is a giant library of <b>pages</b> joined by <b>hyperlinks</b>. Every page has an address called a <b>URL</b>, like a postal address. Computers, however, only understand number addresses (IP addresses), so a helper called <b>DNS</b> works like a phone directory: you give it a name such as google.com and it returns the number.</p><p>To read pages you need a <b>browser</b> (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari) – the reading glasses. To find pages whose address you do not know you use a <b>search engine</b> (Google, Bing) – the library catalogue. The company that gives you the connection, like a water-pipe supplier, is your <b>ISP</b>.</p>", key:"Internet = the roads; WWW = one service on them; URL = address; DNS = phone directory; browser = reader; search engine = catalogue."},
  {k:"tl", title:"Milestones of the internet and web", rows:[
    ["1969","ARPANET, funded by the US Department of Defense, links its first computers – the ancestor of the internet"],
    ["1971","Ray Tomlinson sends the first network e-mail using the @ sign"],
    ["1974","Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn publish the TCP/IP design – 'fathers of the internet'"],
    ["1983","ARPANET switches to TCP/IP (1 January 1983); DNS is introduced the same year"],
    ["1989","Tim Berners-Lee proposes the World Wide Web at CERN (Geneva, Switzerland)"],
    ["1990","Berners-Lee writes the first browser (WorldWideWeb) and the first web server"],
    ["1991","The web is opened to the public; first website: info.cern.ch"],
    ["1993","Mosaic – the first popular graphical browser (NCSA, USA)"],
    ["1994","Netscape Navigator browser released"],
    ["1995","Internet Explorer released; VSNL starts public internet in India (15 August 1995)"],
    ["1998","Google founded by Larry Page and Sergey Brin"],
    ["2008","Google Chrome browser released"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Parts of a URL: https://www.example.gov.in/exams/result.html", head:["Part","In the example","What it is"], rows:[
    ["Protocol (scheme)","https://","Rules used to fetch the page (http, https, ftp)"],
    ["Sub-domain","www","Server name inside the domain"],
    ["Domain name","example.gov.in","Name of the website, turned into an IP address by DNS"],
    ["Top-level domain (TLD)","in","Last part of the domain – country or category"],
    ["Path","/exams/","Folder on the server"],
    ["File name","result.html","The exact page requested"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Common top-level domains", head:["TLD","Meant for","Type"], rows:[
    [".com","Commercial organisations","Generic"],
    [".org","Organisations, often non-profit","Generic"],
    [".net","Network providers (now open to all)","Generic"],
    [".edu","Educational institutions (mainly US)","Sponsored"],
    [".gov","Government (US); India uses .gov.in","Sponsored"],
    [".mil","US military","Sponsored"],
    [".int","International treaty organisations","Sponsored"],
    [".in","India","Country code (ccTLD)"],
    [".uk / .us / .jp","United Kingdom / USA / Japan","Country code (ccTLD)"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Look-alike web terms", head:["Term","Meaning","Example / note"], rows:[
    ["Web page","One single document on the web","result.html"],
    ["Website","A collection of related web pages under one domain","ssc.gov.in"],
    ["Home page","The first / main page of a website (also the page a browser opens at start)","index.html"],
    ["Hyperlink","Clickable text or image that takes you to another page or place","Usually blue and underlined"],
    ["Web browser","Software to view web pages","Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari, Opera"],
    ["Search engine","Website that searches the web for you","Google, Bing, Yahoo, DuckDuckGo"],
    ["Web server","Computer that stores websites and sends pages on request","Apache, IIS"],
    ["Cookie","Small text file a website stores in your browser to remember you","Login, cart, preferences"],
    ["Cache","Stored copies of pages and images so they load faster next time","Clear it if a page looks old"],
    ["History","List of pages you visited","Ctrl+H in Chrome, Edge, Firefox"],
    ["Bookmark / Favourite","Saved link to a page","Ctrl+D in Chrome, Edge, Firefox"],
    ["Download / Upload","Internet to your device / your device to the internet","Saving a PDF vs attaching a photo"],
    ["Streaming","Playing audio or video while it is still arriving, without saving the full file first","YouTube, online radio"],
    ["Intranet","Private network of one organisation using internet technology","Staff-only portal"],
    ["Extranet","Intranet opened to selected outsiders (partners, suppliers)","Dealer portal"]]},
  {k:"facts", title:"HTML and IP basics", rows:[
    ["HTML","Hyper Text Markup Language – the language web pages are written in (Tim Berners-Lee)"],
    ["Markup, not programming","HTML describes structure with tags; it does not do calculations"],
    ["Tags","Written in angle brackets: &lt;html&gt;, &lt;head&gt;, &lt;title&gt;, &lt;body&gt;, &lt;p&gt;, &lt;a&gt; (link), &lt;img&gt;, &lt;br&gt;"],
    ["Container vs empty tag","&lt;p&gt;…&lt;/p&gt; has a closing tag; &lt;br&gt; and &lt;img&gt; have none"],
    ["File extension",".html or .htm"],
    ["Styling / behaviour","CSS gives the look; JavaScript adds interactivity"],
    ["IP address","Unique number for each device on a network, e.g. 192.168.1.1 (IPv4)"],
    ["ISP","Internet Service Provider – BSNL, Jio, Airtel"],
    ["Domain names managed by","ICANN (worldwide); .in registry run by NIXI"]]},
  {k:"trick", h:"URL order: <span class='mn'>P</span>lease <span class='mn'>D</span>on't <span class='mn'>P</span>ush <span class='mn'>F</span>riends = <b>P</b>rotocol, <b>D</b>omain, <b>P</b>ath, <b>F</b>ile. Web father: <span class='mn'>T</span>im = <span class='mn'>T</span>hree W's; internet fathers: <span class='mn'>C</span>erf &amp; <span class='mn'>K</span>ahn = <b>TCP</b>."},
  {k:"trap", h:"<b>Internet ≠ WWW</b>: the internet is the network (1969, ARPANET); the web is a service on it (1989–91, Berners-Lee). <b>Browser ≠ search engine</b>: Chrome is a browser, Google is a search engine. <b>Web page ≠ website ≠ home page</b>. <b>Cookie ≠ cache</b>: cookie remembers <i>you</i>, cache stores <i>page files</i>. <b>Intranet</b> is private, <b>extranet</b> lets chosen outsiders in."}
], sum:["Internet is the global network (ARPANET 1969); the WWW is a service on it, invented by Tim Berners-Lee at CERN in 1989-91.","A URL has protocol, domain name (ending in a TLD such as .com, .gov, .in) and path; DNS converts the name into an IP address.","Browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari; historic Mosaic, Netscape) display pages; search engines (Google, Bing) find them.","HTML (Hyper Text Markup Language) builds web pages with tags; files end in .html or .htm.","Cookies remember the user, cache stores page copies; intranet is private, extranet admits chosen outsiders; an ISP gives the connection."]},

{id:"C13", g:"cmp", heat:2, title:"E-mail & e-banking", hi:"ई-मेल एवं ई-बैंकिंग", blocks:[
  {k:"hot", h:"Email questions focus on <b>CC vs BCC</b>, the folders (Drafts, Outbox, Spam) and the inventor <b>Ray Tomlinson</b>. Banking questions ask the full forms and differences of <b>NEFT, RTGS, IMPS, UPI</b> and safe-payment habits (OTP, PIN)."},
  {k:"story", h:"<p><b>E-mail</b> is a post office that works in seconds. Your e-mail address is your letter box: the part before <b>@</b> is your name on the box, the part after it is the post office (the mail server) that keeps the box, as in ravi@gmail.com.</p><p>When you write a letter you can put people in the <b>To</b> line (the main readers), in <b>CC</b> (a copy for information, everyone can see it) or in <b>BCC</b> (a hidden copy – other readers never learn this person got it). Unfinished letters wait in <b>Drafts</b>; letters still leaving sit in the <b>Outbox</b>; letters already gone appear in <b>Sent</b>; junk lands in <b>Spam</b>.</p><p><b>E-banking</b> is the same idea for money. Instead of standing in a bank queue, you move money with net banking, a mobile app or <b>UPI</b>. Each system is a different road: <b>RTGS</b> for big amounts instantly, <b>NEFT</b> in half-hourly batches, <b>IMPS</b> and <b>UPI</b> instantly at any hour. The keys to your money are your PIN and OTP – never share them with anyone.</p>", key:"@ splits user and mail server; BCC hides recipients; RTGS big and instant, NEFT batches, IMPS and UPI instant 24x7; never share PIN or OTP."},
  {k:"table", title:"E-mail fields and folders", head:["Item","What it does","Remember"], rows:[
    ["To","Main recipient(s)","Must have at least one recipient in To, CC or BCC"],
    ["CC (Carbon Copy)","Sends a copy; all recipients can see CC addresses","For information only"],
    ["BCC (Blind Carbon Copy)","Sends a copy; BCC addresses are hidden from every other recipient","Use for mass mails to protect privacy"],
    ["Subject","One-line topic of the mail","Shown in the inbox list"],
    ["Attachment","File sent with the mail (paper-clip icon)","Gmail limit 25 MB; bigger files go as a Google Drive link"],
    ["Signature","Text added automatically at the end of every mail","Name, designation, phone"],
    ["Inbox","Received mail","Unread mail usually in bold"],
    ["Outbox","Mail waiting to be sent (e.g. when offline)","Not yet gone"],
    ["Sent","Copies of mail already sent","Proof you sent it"],
    ["Drafts","Saved, unfinished mail – not sent","Auto-saved while typing"],
    ["Spam / Junk","Unwanted or suspicious bulk mail","Filtered automatically"],
    ["Trash / Bin","Deleted mail","Gmail empties it after 30 days"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Reply, Reply All, Forward", head:["Action","Goes to","Attachments"], rows:[
    ["Reply","Only the original sender","Original attachment is not sent back"],
    ["Reply All","Sender plus everyone in To and CC (never the BCC people)","Not sent back"],
    ["Forward","Anyone new you type in","Original attachments are included"]]},
  {k:"facts", title:"E-mail facts", rows:[
    ["Inventor of network e-mail","Ray Tomlinson, 1971, on ARPANET"],
    ["Why @","Tomlinson chose @ to separate the user name from the host (computer) name"],
    ["Address format","username@domain, e.g. ravi@gmail.com"],
    ["Sending protocol","SMTP (port 25)"],
    ["Receiving protocols","POP3 (downloads, port 110) and IMAP (keeps mail synced on server, port 143)"],
    ["Hotmail","Free web mail founded in 1996 by Sabeer Bhatia and Jack Smith"],
    ["Gmail","Launched by Google on 1 April 2004"],
    ["Gmail free storage","15 GB, shared with Google Drive and Google Photos"],
    ["Gmail features","Labels, Undo Send, Schedule send, Confidential mode, Spam filter"],
    ["Spam","Unsolicited bulk e-mail"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Electronic fund transfer systems", head:["System","Full form","Run by","Key point"], rows:[
    ["NEFT","National Electronic Funds Transfer","RBI","Settled in half-hourly batches; no minimum amount; available 24x7 since December 2019"],
    ["RTGS","Real Time Gross Settlement","RBI","Each transfer settled individually in real time; minimum Rs 2 lakh, no upper limit; 24x7 since December 2020"],
    ["IMPS","Immediate Payment Service","NPCI","Instant, 24x7 including holidays; launched 2010"],
    ["UPI","Unified Payments Interface","NPCI","Instant mobile payments using a virtual address (name@bank) or QR code; launched 2016"],
    ["BHIM","Bharat Interface for Money","NPCI","Government-backed UPI app, launched December 2016"],
    ["RuPay","India's own card network","NPCI","Domestic alternative to Visa / Mastercard"]]},
  {k:"facts", title:"Safe digital banking", rows:[
    ["OTP","One Time Password – valid once, for a short time"],
    ["2FA","Two-factor authentication: something you know (password) + something you have (phone/OTP)"],
    ["PIN","Personal Identification Number – never write it on the card, never share it"],
    ["UPI PIN","Needed only to SEND money; you never enter a PIN to RECEIVE money"],
    ["Banks never ask for","Password, PIN, OTP or CVV by call, SMS or e-mail"],
    ["Safe website","Starts with https:// and shows a padlock"],
    ["Public Wi-Fi","Avoid doing banking on open public Wi-Fi"],
    ["IFSC","Indian Financial System Code – 11-character bank branch code needed for NEFT/RTGS"],
    ["KYC","Know Your Customer – identity check by banks"],
    ["Fraud reporting","Cyber-crime helpline 1930 and portal cybercrime.gov.in"]]},
  {k:"trick", h:"<span class='mn'>B</span>CC = <span class='mn'>B</span>lind = nobody sees. Money speed: <span class='mn'>R</span>TGS = <span class='mn'>R</span>ich (Rs 2 lakh minimum) and <span class='mn'>R</span>eal time; <span class='mn'>N</span>EFT = <span class='mn'>N</span>ot instant (batches); <span class='mn'>I</span>MPS = <span class='mn'>I</span>mmediate."},
  {k:"trap", h:"<b>CC vs BCC</b>: both get the mail; only BCC is hidden. <b>Outbox vs Sent</b>: Outbox = not yet gone. <b>Drafts</b> are never sent. <b>Reply All</b> does not reach BCC recipients. <b>RTGS vs NEFT</b>: RTGS is real-time and individual with a Rs 2 lakh minimum; NEFT is in batches. <b>UPI and IMPS</b> are run by NPCI, NEFT and RTGS by RBI. You never need a PIN to <i>receive</i> money."}
], sum:["An e-mail address is username@domain; Ray Tomlinson sent the first network e-mail in 1971 and chose the @ sign.","CC recipients are visible to all; BCC (Blind Carbon Copy) recipients are hidden from others.","Drafts are unsent saved mails, Outbox holds mails waiting to go, Sent keeps copies of sent mails, Spam holds junk.","NEFT settles in half-hourly batches, RTGS settles each transfer in real time (minimum Rs 2 lakh); both run by RBI.","IMPS and UPI (NPCI) are instant 24x7; never share PIN, OTP or CVV - banks never ask for them."]},

{id:"C14", g:"cmp", heat:3, title:"Networking basics", hi:"नेटवर्किंग की मूल बातें", blocks:[
  {k:"hot", h:"Expect 1–3 questions: <b>LAN/MAN/WAN</b>, which <b>topology</b> needs a central hub, what a <b>router / switch / modem</b> does, the <b>OSI layers</b> in order, <b>IPv4 vs IPv6</b> bits, and which cable connects two computers (<b>Ethernet</b>)."},
  {k:"story", h:"<p>A <b>network</b> is simply two or more computers joined so they can share files, printers and the internet – like houses in a colony joined by lanes so neighbours can share things.</p><p>Size gives the name: a <b>PAN</b> is around one person (phone to earphones by Bluetooth), a <b>LAN</b> covers a room or building, a <b>MAN</b> covers a city, and a <b>WAN</b> spans countries – the internet is the biggest WAN.</p><p>The <b>topology</b> is the shape of the lanes: one long road (bus), everyone connected to a central chowk (star), a circle (ring) or every house joined to every other (mesh). Devices act like traffic staff: a <b>hub</b> shouts every message to everyone, a <b>switch</b> delivers it only to the right house, a <b>router</b> chooses the route between colonies (networks), and a <b>modem</b> converts the computer's digital signal for the telephone or cable line.</p><p>Every computer has a fixed hardware name, the <b>MAC address</b>, and a network address, the <b>IP address</b>.</p>", key:"Network = joined computers; PAN-LAN-MAN-WAN by size; topology = shape; hub broadcasts, switch delivers, router routes, modem converts."},
  {k:"table", title:"Types of network by size", head:["Type","Full form","Range","Example"], rows:[
    ["PAN","Personal Area Network","A few metres around one person","Phone with Bluetooth earphones"],
    ["LAN","Local Area Network","Room, building or campus","Office or school computer lab"],
    ["CAN","Campus Area Network","Several buildings of one campus","University campus"],
    ["MAN","Metropolitan Area Network","A city","City-wide cable TV network"],
    ["WAN","Wide Area Network","Country or world","The internet (the largest WAN)"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Network topologies", head:["Topology","Layout","Plus","Minus"], rows:[
    ["Bus","All nodes on one backbone cable with terminators at the ends","Cheap, least cable","Backbone break stops the whole network; collisions"],
    ["Star","Every node connected to a central hub or switch","Easy to add nodes and find faults; one cable failure affects only one node","Central device fails = whole network fails"],
    ["Ring","Each node connected to two neighbours in a closed loop; data passes round (token passing)","Orderly, no collisions","One broken node or link can stop the ring"],
    ["Mesh","Every node connected to every other node: n(n-1)/2 links","Most reliable, many alternate paths","Most cable and cost"],
    ["Tree","Hierarchy of star networks joined to a backbone","Easy to expand","Root/backbone failure hits branches"],
    ["Hybrid","Mix of two or more topologies","Flexible","Complex to design"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Network devices", head:["Device","What it does","OSI layer"], rows:[
    ["NIC (Network Interface Card)","Connects a computer to a network; carries the MAC address","Data link (and physical)"],
    ["Repeater","Regenerates (amplifies) a weak signal to extend distance","Physical"],
    ["Hub","Sends incoming data to ALL ports – not intelligent","Physical"],
    ["Bridge","Joins two LAN segments and filters by MAC address","Data link"],
    ["Switch","Sends data only to the port of the destination MAC address – intelligent","Data link"],
    ["Router","Connects different networks and chooses the best path using IP addresses","Network"],
    ["Gateway","Connects networks that use different protocols (protocol converter)","Can work at all layers"],
    ["Modem","MOdulator-DEModulator: digital to analog and back over phone/cable lines","Physical"],
    ["Access point","Lets wireless (Wi-Fi) devices join a wired network","Data link"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Transmission media", head:["Medium","Type","Key fact"], rows:[
    ["Twisted pair","Wired (guided)","Copper pairs twisted to reduce interference; used in Ethernet LAN and telephone lines; RJ-45 connector for LAN, RJ-11 for phone"],
    ["Coaxial cable","Wired (guided)","Central copper core with shield; cable TV"],
    ["Optical fibre","Wired (guided)","Carries LIGHT by total internal reflection; fastest, highest bandwidth, immune to electrical interference"],
    ["Radio waves","Wireless (unguided)","Radio, Wi-Fi, mobile networks"],
    ["Microwave","Wireless (unguided)","Line-of-sight towers and satellite links"],
    ["Infrared","Wireless (unguided)","Very short range, line-of-sight: TV remote"],
    ["Bluetooth","Wireless (unguided)","Short-range PAN, IEEE 802.15.1"],
    ["Wi-Fi","Wireless (unguided)","Wireless LAN, IEEE 802.11 (Ethernet LAN is IEEE 802.3)"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"OSI model – 7 layers (bottom to top)", head:["No.","Layer","Main job"], rows:[
    ["1","Physical","Sends raw bits over cable or air"],
    ["2","Data link","Frames, MAC addresses, error detection on one link"],
    ["3","Network","IP addressing and routing between networks (packets)"],
    ["4","Transport","End-to-end delivery, segments; TCP and UDP"],
    ["5","Session","Opens, manages and closes sessions"],
    ["6","Presentation","Translation, encryption, compression"],
    ["7","Application","Services for the user: HTTP, FTP, SMTP, DNS"]]},
  {k:"facts", title:"Addresses, speed and modes", rows:[
    ["OSI developed by","ISO (International Organization for Standardization)"],
    ["TCP/IP model layers","4: Network access (link), Internet, Transport, Application"],
    ["IPv4","32 bits, 4 numbers (octets) of 0–255, dotted decimal, e.g. 192.168.1.1; about 4.3 billion (2^32) addresses"],
    ["IPv6","128 bits, 8 groups of 4 hex digits separated by colons; about 3.4 x 10^38 addresses"],
    ["MAC address","48 bits = 6 bytes = 12 hex digits; burnt into the NIC; physical address"],
    ["IP vs MAC","IP = logical, can change; MAC = physical, fixed"],
    ["Bandwidth","Data-carrying capacity; measured in bps (bits per second): Kbps, Mbps, Gbps"],
    ["Simplex","One direction only – radio/TV broadcast, keyboard to computer"],
    ["Half duplex","Both directions, one at a time – walkie-talkie"],
    ["Full duplex","Both directions at the same time – telephone call"],
    ["Client-server","Central server provides services to clients – a bank's network"],
    ["Peer-to-peer (P2P)","All computers equal; each can share and request"],
    ["Connect two computers","Ethernet cable (a crossover Ethernet cable in older direct PC-to-PC links)"]]},
  {k:"trick", h:"OSI bottom-up: <span class='mn'>P</span>lease <span class='mn'>D</span>o <span class='mn'>N</span>ot <span class='mn'>T</span>hrow <span class='mn'>S</span>ausage <span class='mn'>P</span>izza <span class='mn'>A</span>way. Top-down: <span class='mn'>A</span>ll <span class='mn'>P</span>eople <span class='mn'>S</span>eem <span class='mn'>T</span>o <span class='mn'>N</span>eed <span class='mn'>D</span>ata <span class='mn'>P</span>rocessing. Size: <span class='mn'>P</span>-<span class='mn'>L</span>-<span class='mn'>M</span>-<span class='mn'>W</span> = 'Please Let Me Win'."},
  {k:"trap", h:"<b>Hub vs switch</b>: hub sends to all, switch sends to the right port. <b>Switch vs router</b>: switch works inside one network (MAC), router joins networks (IP). <b>Modem vs router</b>: modem converts signals, router directs traffic. <b>Star vs bus</b>: star dies when the hub dies; bus dies when the backbone breaks. <b>IPv4 32 bits, IPv6 128 bits, MAC 48 bits</b> – don't mix them. Bandwidth is in <b>bps</b>, not bytes."}
], sum:["A network joins computers to share resources; by size: PAN, LAN, CAN, MAN, WAN (the internet is the largest WAN).","Topologies: bus (one backbone), star (central hub), ring (loop), mesh (all-to-all, n(n-1)/2 links), tree and hybrid.","Hub broadcasts, switch delivers by MAC, router joins networks by IP, modem converts digital and analog, repeater boosts signals.","OSI has 7 layers: Physical, Data link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application; TCP/IP has 4.","IPv4 is 32-bit, IPv6 is 128-bit, MAC is 48-bit; optical fibre carries light; an Ethernet cable connects two computers."]},

{id:"C15", g:"cmp", heat:3, title:"Protocols, full forms & abbreviations", hi:"प्रोटोकॉल, पूर्ण रूप एवं संक्षिप्ताक्षर", blocks:[
  {k:"hot", h:"Full forms are the single most common computer question type in SSC and RRB papers: <b>HTTP, HTML, URL, VIRUS, CAPTCHA, DNS, SMTP, UPI</b>. Protocol-to-job and well-known <b>port numbers</b> appear in Tier 2."},
  {k:"story", h:"<p>A <b>protocol</b> is a set of agreed rules for talking. When two people meet they follow a protocol: greet, speak in turn, say goodbye. Computers from different companies can talk only because they follow the same written rules.</p><p>Each job has its own protocol. <b>HTTP</b> fetches web pages, <b>HTTPS</b> does the same with encryption, <b>FTP</b> moves files, <b>SMTP</b> sends mail while <b>POP3</b> and <b>IMAP</b> receive it, <b>DNS</b> looks up addresses and <b>DHCP</b> hands out IP addresses automatically, like a receptionist giving room numbers to guests.</p><p>A single computer runs many services at once, so each service listens at a numbered door called a <b>port</b>: web at 80, secure web at 443, mail sending at 25. Finally, computing is full of short forms. Learn them in groups – internet, networking, files, banking, security, new technology – and they stop looking like alphabet soup.</p>", key:"Protocol = agreed rules; each service has its protocol and port number; learn full forms in groups."},
  {k:"table", title:"Main protocols and their ports", head:["Protocol","Full form","Job","Port"], rows:[
    ["HTTP","Hyper Text Transfer Protocol","Transfers web pages","80"],
    ["HTTPS","Hyper Text Transfer Protocol Secure","Encrypted web pages (padlock), uses SSL/TLS","443"],
    ["FTP","File Transfer Protocol","Uploads/downloads files between computers","20 (data), 21 (control)"],
    ["SMTP","Simple Mail Transfer Protocol","SENDS e-mail","25"],
    ["POP3","Post Office Protocol version 3","Receives e-mail by downloading it to the device","110"],
    ["IMAP","Internet Message Access Protocol","Receives e-mail, keeping it on the server in sync across devices","143"],
    ["DNS","Domain Name System","Converts domain names into IP addresses","53"],
    ["Telnet","–","Remote login, unencrypted","23"],
    ["SSH","Secure Shell","Encrypted remote login","22"],
    ["DHCP","Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol","Automatically assigns IP addresses to devices","–"],
    ["ARP","Address Resolution Protocol","Finds the MAC address for a known IP address","–"],
    ["TCP","Transmission Control Protocol","Reliable, connection-oriented delivery","–"],
    ["UDP","User Datagram Protocol","Fast, connectionless, no delivery guarantee","–"],
    ["IP","Internet Protocol","Addresses and routes packets","–"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"TCP vs UDP", head:["Point","TCP","UDP"], rows:[
    ["Connection","Connection-oriented (handshake first)","Connectionless"],
    ["Reliability","Guaranteed, in-order delivery; resends lost data","No guarantee, no resending"],
    ["Speed","Slower","Faster"],
    ["Used for","Web (HTTP), e-mail, file transfer","Live video, online games, voice calls, DNS queries"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Full forms – internet and networking", head:["Short","Full form"], rows:[
    ["WWW","World Wide Web"],["URL","Uniform Resource Locator"],["HTML","Hyper Text Markup Language"],["XML","eXtensible Markup Language"],["CSS","Cascading Style Sheets"],["ISP","Internet Service Provider"],["IP","Internet Protocol"],["LAN","Local Area Network"],["MAN","Metropolitan Area Network"],["WAN","Wide Area Network"],["PAN","Personal Area Network"],["WLAN","Wireless Local Area Network"],["NIC","Network Interface Card"],["MAC","Media Access Control"],["OSI","Open Systems Interconnection"],["Modem","Modulator-Demodulator"],["VPN","Virtual Private Network"],["VoIP","Voice over Internet Protocol"],["Wi-Fi","Wireless Fidelity (popular expansion)"],["ARPANET","Advanced Research Projects Agency Network"],["ICANN","Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers"],["DSL","Digital Subscriber Line"],["ISDN","Integrated Services Digital Network"],["NAT","Network Address Translation"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Full forms – files, interface and devices", head:["Short","Full form"], rows:[
    ["GUI","Graphical User Interface"],["CLI","Command Line Interface"],["OS","Operating System"],["PDF","Portable Document Format"],["JPEG","Joint Photographic Experts Group"],["GIF","Graphics Interchange Format"],["PNG","Portable Network Graphics"],["MPEG","Moving Picture Experts Group"],["MP3","MPEG Audio Layer 3"],["GPS","Global Positioning System"],["SIM","Subscriber Identity Module"],["GSM","Global System for Mobile Communications"],["CDMA","Code Division Multiple Access"],["LTE","Long Term Evolution"],["QR code","Quick Response code"],["NFC","Near Field Communication"],["RFID","Radio Frequency Identification"],["BCC / CC","Blind Carbon Copy / Carbon Copy"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Full forms – security, banking and new tech", head:["Short","Full form"], rows:[
    ["VIRUS","Vital Information Resources Under Siege"],["CAPTCHA","Completely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart"],["OTP","One Time Password"],["PIN","Personal Identification Number"],["SSL","Secure Sockets Layer"],["TLS","Transport Layer Security"],["DoS / DDoS","Denial of Service / Distributed Denial of Service"],["2FA / MFA","Two-Factor / Multi-Factor Authentication"],["CERT-In","Indian Computer Emergency Response Team"],["UPI","Unified Payments Interface"],["NPCI","National Payments Corporation of India"],["BHIM","Bharat Interface for Money"],["NEFT","National Electronic Funds Transfer"],["RTGS","Real Time Gross Settlement"],["IMPS","Immediate Payment Service"],["KYC","Know Your Customer"],["IFSC","Indian Financial System Code"],["ATM","Automated Teller Machine"],["POS","Point of Sale"],["CVV","Card Verification Value"],["IoT","Internet of Things"],["AI / ML","Artificial Intelligence / Machine Learning"],["AR / VR","Augmented Reality / Virtual Reality"],["SaaS","Software as a Service"],["PaaS","Platform as a Service"],["IaaS","Infrastructure as a Service"],["UIDAI","Unique Identification Authority of India"],["C-DAC","Centre for Development of Advanced Computing"]]},
  {k:"trick", h:"Mail: <span class='mn'>S</span>MTP = <span class='mn'>S</span>end; <span class='mn'>P</span>OP = <span class='mn'>P</span>ull down (download); <span class='mn'>I</span>MAP = keep <span class='mn'>I</span>n place on server. Ports: FTP <b>21</b> &lt; SSH <b>22</b> &lt; Telnet <b>23</b> &lt; SMTP <b>25</b> – the '20s ladder'; DNS <b>53</b>, HTTP <b>80</b>, POP3 <b>110</b>, IMAP <b>143</b>, HTTPS <b>443</b>."},
  {k:"trap", h:"<b>HTTP</b> is Hyper Text <i>Transfer</i> Protocol; <b>HTML</b> is Hyper Text <i>Markup</i> Language – exams swap Transfer/Transport/Markup. <b>URL</b> is <i>Uniform</i> (not Universal) Resource Locator. <b>SMTP sends, POP3/IMAP receive</b>. <b>TCP</b> is connection-oriented, <b>UDP</b> connectionless. <b>DNS</b> = name to IP; <b>ARP</b> = IP to MAC; <b>DHCP</b> = gives out IPs."}
], sum:["A protocol is a set of rules for communication; HTTP (port 80) carries web pages, HTTPS (port 443) does it securely.","FTP (20/21) moves files; SMTP (25) sends mail; POP3 (110) and IMAP (143) receive mail; DNS (53) turns names into IP addresses.","TCP is reliable and connection-oriented; UDP is fast and connectionless; DHCP assigns IP addresses, ARP finds MAC from IP.","HTTP = Hyper Text Transfer Protocol, HTML = Hyper Text Markup Language, URL = Uniform Resource Locator.","VIRUS = Vital Information Resources Under Siege; CAPTCHA tells computers and humans apart; UPI = Unified Payments Interface."]},

{id:"C16", g:"cmp", heat:3, title:"Cyber security", hi:"साइबर सुरक्षा", blocks:[
  {k:"hot", h:"The SSC syllabus names <b>network and information security threats and preventive measures</b>. Expect the difference between <b>virus, worm and Trojan</b>, attacks like <b>phishing</b> and <b>DDoS</b>, and what a <b>firewall</b> or <b>encryption</b> does."},
  {k:"story", h:"<p>Think of your computer as a house. <b>Malware</b> means any harmful software that gets inside. A <b>virus</b> hides inside a genuine file and spreads only when that file is opened, like a germ on a shared towel. A <b>worm</b> crawls from house to house by itself through the network. A <b>Trojan horse</b> arrives as a gift – a free game – with thieves hidden inside. <b>Ransomware</b> locks your cupboards and asks for money for the key. <b>Spyware</b> and <b>keyloggers</b> are hidden cameras recording what you do and type.</p><p>Criminals also simply trick people. <b>Phishing</b> is a fake e-mail from 'your bank' asking for your password; the same trick by phone call is <b>vishing</b> and by SMS is <b>smishing</b>.</p><p>Protection works in layers like locks, guards and alarms: a <b>firewall</b> is the gatekeeper checking traffic, <b>antivirus</b> is the guard who catches known intruders, <b>encryption</b> turns data into secret code, and <b>two-factor authentication</b> asks for a second key such as an OTP. Regular updates and backups complete the safety plan.</p>", key:"Virus needs a host, worm spreads alone, Trojan disguises itself; phishing tricks people; firewall, antivirus, encryption, 2FA, updates and backups protect."},
  {k:"table", title:"Types of malware", head:["Malware","How it behaves","One-line difference"], rows:[
    ["Virus","Attaches to a program or file and runs when that file is opened","Needs a host file and human action to spread"],
    ["Worm","Copies itself across networks automatically","Self-replicating, needs NO host file"],
    ["Trojan horse","Pretends to be useful software but opens a back door","Disguised; does NOT replicate itself"],
    ["Ransomware","Encrypts or locks your files and demands payment (WannaCry, 2017)","Holds data hostage"],
    ["Spyware","Secretly collects your information and activity","Spies silently"],
    ["Adware","Floods the screen with unwanted advertisements","Shows ads"],
    ["Keylogger","Records every key you press to steal passwords","Captures keystrokes"],
    ["Rootkit","Hides deep in the system and gives the attacker admin-level control","Hard to detect"],
    ["Bot / Botnet","Infected 'zombie' computer controlled remotely; many together form a botnet","Used for spam and DDoS attacks"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Common attacks", head:["Attack","What happens"], rows:[
    ["Phishing","Fake e-mails or websites that look genuine to steal passwords or card details"],
    ["Vishing","Phishing by voice / phone call (Voice + phishing)"],
    ["Smishing","Phishing by SMS text message"],
    ["Spoofing","Faking the identity of a trusted sender – e-mail address, caller ID, IP address or website"],
    ["Pharming","Redirecting users from a real website to a fake one, e.g. by poisoning DNS – even a correctly typed address leads to the fake site"],
    ["DoS","Denial of Service – flooding a server with traffic so real users cannot reach it"],
    ["DDoS","Distributed DoS – the flood comes from many computers (a botnet) at once"],
    ["Man-in-the-middle","Attacker secretly sits between two parties and reads or changes their messages"],
    ["SQL injection","Malicious database commands typed into a website's input box"],
    ["Brute force","Trying every possible password until one works"],
    ["Social engineering","Manipulating people (not machines) into revealing secrets"],
    ["Identity theft","Using someone's personal details to commit fraud"],
    ["Spam","Unwanted bulk messages, often carrying phishing links"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Preventive measures", head:["Measure","What it does"], rows:[
    ["Antivirus","Detects and removes known malware (e.g. Quick Heal, Norton, Microsoft Defender)"],
    ["Firewall","Hardware or software that filters incoming and outgoing network traffic by rules – a security guard at the gate"],
    ["Encryption","Converts plaintext into unreadable ciphertext; only the right key decrypts it"],
    ["Strong password","Long; mix of capital and small letters, numbers and symbols; not your name or birth date"],
    ["2FA / MFA","Second proof besides the password, such as an OTP, fingerprint or authenticator app"],
    ["Updates and patches","Fix security holes found in software"],
    ["Backups","Copies of data to recover after ransomware or crash"],
    ["CAPTCHA","Test that blocks automated bots from filling forms"],
    ["HTTPS padlock","Shows the connection to the site is encrypted"],
    ["Digital signature","Proves who sent a document and that it was not altered"],
    ["Digital certificate","Electronic ID of a website or person issued by a Certifying Authority"]]},
  {k:"facts", title:"Terms and Indian law", rows:[
    ["White hat hacker","Ethical hacker who tests systems with permission"],
    ["Black hat hacker","Criminal hacker who breaks in for harm or profit"],
    ["Grey hat hacker","Breaks in without permission but without bad intent"],
    ["Symmetric encryption","One same key to lock and unlock"],
    ["Asymmetric encryption","Public key to encrypt, private key to decrypt"],
    ["Digital signature","Signed with the sender's private key, verified with the public key"],
    ["First PC virus (MS-DOS)","Brain, 1986, written in Pakistan"],
    ["IT Act","Information Technology Act, 2000 – India's cyber law; gave legal status to e-records and digital signatures"],
    ["IT (Amendment) Act","2008 – added cyber terrorism (Section 66F) and more cyber offences"],
    ["CERT-In","Indian Computer Emergency Response Team – national agency for cyber incidents, under MeitY, set up 2004"],
    ["Cyber-crime helpline","1930"],
    ["Cyber-crime reporting portal","cybercrime.gov.in"]]},
  {k:"trick", h:"Malware by legs: <span class='mn'>V</span>irus = <span class='mn'>V</span>ehicle needed (host file); <span class='mn'>W</span>orm = <span class='mn'>W</span>alks alone; <span class='mn'>T</span>rojan = <span class='mn'>T</span>ricks you with a gift. Phishing family by channel: <span class='mn'>E</span>-mail = Phishing, <span class='mn'>V</span>oice = Vishing, <span class='mn'>S</span>MS = Smishing."},
  {k:"trap", h:"<b>Virus vs worm</b>: only the worm spreads without a host. <b>Trojan</b> does not replicate. <b>Phishing vs pharming</b>: phishing needs you to click a fake link; pharming redirects you even when you type the right address. <b>DoS vs DDoS</b>: DDoS uses many machines. <b>Firewall vs antivirus</b>: firewall filters traffic, antivirus removes malware. <b>Cookies vs cache</b>: cookies store your details, cache stores page files. <b>Encryption ≠ compression</b>."}
], sum:["Malware: virus needs a host file, worm self-replicates over networks, Trojan disguises itself, ransomware demands money, spyware and keyloggers steal data.","Phishing (e-mail), vishing (voice), smishing (SMS) trick people; pharming redirects to fake sites; DDoS floods a server from many computers.","A firewall filters network traffic; antivirus removes malware; encryption turns plaintext into ciphertext.","Strong passwords, 2FA/MFA, updates, backups and HTTPS help prevent attacks; white hat hackers are ethical.","India: IT Act 2000 (amended 2008), CERT-In for cyber incidents, helpline 1930 and cybercrime.gov.in."]},

{id:"C17", g:"cmp", heat:2, title:"New technologies & computer terms", hi:"नई तकनीकें एवं कंप्यूटर शब्दावली", blocks:[
  {k:"hot", h:"Papers now ask one-liners on <b>AI, cloud computing (SaaS/PaaS/IaaS), IoT, blockchain</b>, plus 'who founded / who created' questions such as <b>father of AI</b> (John McCarthy coined the term) and India's <b>Digital India, DigiLocker, PARAM</b>."},
  {k:"story", h:"<p>New technology words sound frightening but each has a simple idea. <b>Artificial Intelligence</b> means making machines do things that need human-like thinking, such as recognising faces or answering questions. <b>Machine learning</b> is AI that learns from examples instead of fixed rules – the way a child learns what a dog looks like after seeing many dogs. <b>Generative AI</b> creates new text, pictures or music; chatbots are one example.</p><p><b>Cloud computing</b> is renting computing power over the internet like taking a taxi instead of buying a car: you can rent ready software (SaaS), a platform to build on (PaaS) or bare infrastructure (IaaS). The <b>Internet of Things</b> connects everyday objects – fans, meters, watches – to the internet.</p><p>A <b>blockchain</b> is a record book copied on thousands of computers so no single person can secretly change it; Bitcoin was built on it. <b>AR</b> adds digital things to the real view, while <b>VR</b> replaces the real view completely with a digital world.</p>", key:"AI thinks, ML learns from data, cloud = renting computing, IoT = connected objects, blockchain = shared unchangeable ledger, AR adds and VR replaces."},
  {k:"table", title:"New technologies in one line", head:["Technology","Meaning","Example"], rows:[
    ["Artificial Intelligence (AI)","Machines performing tasks that need human-like intelligence","Face unlock, voice assistants"],
    ["Machine Learning (ML)","Subset of AI: systems learn patterns from data","Spam filter, recommendations"],
    ["Deep Learning","Subset of ML using many-layered neural networks","Image and speech recognition"],
    ["Generative AI","AI that creates new text, images, audio or code","Chatbots such as ChatGPT (OpenAI, November 2022)"],
    ["Internet of Things (IoT)","Everyday devices with sensors connected to the internet","Smart meter, smartwatch"],
    ["Big Data","Data too large or fast for ordinary tools; described by Volume, Velocity, Variety","Social-media analytics"],
    ["Blockchain","Distributed, tamper-resistant ledger shared across many computers","Cryptocurrency records"],
    ["Cryptocurrency","Digital currency secured by cryptography, usually on a blockchain","Bitcoin (2009, by 'Satoshi Nakamoto')"],
    ["Augmented Reality (AR)","Adds digital objects to the real-world view","Pokemon Go, Snapchat filters"],
    ["Virtual Reality (VR)","Fully computer-made world seen through a headset","VR gaming, flight simulators"],
    ["5G","Fifth-generation mobile network: high speed, very low delay; launched in India in October 2022","Many IoT devices at once"],
    ["Quantum computing","Uses qubits, which can be 0 and 1 at once (superposition)","Research machines"],
    ["Edge computing","Processing data near where it is produced instead of a far data centre","Self-driving car sensors"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Cloud computing", head:["Model","You get","Example"], rows:[
    ["SaaS – Software as a Service","Ready-to-use software through a browser","Gmail, Google Docs, Microsoft 365"],
    ["PaaS – Platform as a Service","Platform and tools to build and run your own apps","Google App Engine"],
    ["IaaS – Infrastructure as a Service","Virtual servers, storage, networking","Amazon EC2 (AWS)"],
    ["Public cloud","Shared by many customers, run by a provider","AWS, Microsoft Azure, Google Cloud"],
    ["Private cloud","Used by one organisation only","Bank's own data centre cloud"],
    ["Hybrid cloud","Mix of public and private","Sensitive data private, rest public"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Famous people of computing", head:["Person","Known for"], rows:[
    ["Charles Babbage","Father of the computer (Analytical Engine)"],
    ["Ada Lovelace","First computer programmer"],
    ["Alan Turing","Father of theoretical computer science and AI; Turing Test (1950)"],
    ["John McCarthy","Coined the term 'Artificial Intelligence' (1955–56, Dartmouth); called the father of AI; created LISP"],
    ["Tim Berners-Lee","Inventor of the World Wide Web"],
    ["Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn","Fathers of the internet (TCP/IP)"],
    ["Ray Tomlinson","Inventor of network e-mail"],
    ["Dennis Ritchie","Created the C language (Bell Labs); co-created UNIX with Ken Thompson"],
    ["James Gosling","Father of Java (Sun Microsystems, 1995)"],
    ["Guido van Rossum","Creator of Python (1991)"],
    ["Linus Torvalds","Created the Linux kernel (1991)"],
    ["Bill Gates and Paul Allen","Founded Microsoft (1975)"],
    ["Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak","Founded Apple (1976)"],
    ["Larry Page and Sergey Brin","Founded Google (1998)"],
    ["Mark Zuckerberg","Founded Facebook (2004)"],
    ["Vijay Bhatkar","Father of Indian supercomputing (PARAM)"]]},
  {k:"tl", title:"India's digital journey", rows:[
    ["1988","C-DAC (Centre for Development of Advanced Computing) set up, Pune"],
    ["1991","PARAM 8000 – India's first supercomputer, by C-DAC"],
    ["2004","CERT-In set up"],
    ["2009","UIDAI set up; Aadhaar (12-digit unique ID) follows from 2010"],
    ["2015","Digital India launched (1 July 2015); DigiLocker launched; National Supercomputing Mission approved"],
    ["2016","UPI launched by NPCI; BHIM app launched in December"],
    ["2022","5G services launched in India (1 October 2022)"]]},
  {k:"trick", h:"Cloud stack from top: <span class='mn'>S</span>aaS = <span class='mn'>S</span>oftware you just use; <span class='mn'>P</span>aaS = <span class='mn'>P</span>latform to build; <span class='mn'>I</span>aaS = <span class='mn'>I</span>ron (bare hardware). AI family: <span class='mn'>A</span> ⊃ <span class='mn'>M</span> ⊃ <span class='mn'>D</span> – 'A Mango Drop' (AI contains ML contains Deep learning)."},
  {k:"trap", h:"<b>Father of AI</b>: John McCarthy coined the term; Alan Turing laid the theory – read the question wording. <b>AR vs VR</b>: AR adds to reality, VR replaces it. <b>ML vs DL</b>: deep learning is a part of ML, not the other way. <b>Gmail is SaaS</b>, AWS EC2 is IaaS. <b>Python</b> is Guido van Rossum, <b>Java</b> is James Gosling, <b>Linux</b> is Linus Torvalds – not the founders of Microsoft or Apple."}
], sum:["AI makes machines think; ML learns from data; deep learning uses neural networks; generative AI creates new content.","Cloud computing: SaaS (Gmail), PaaS (App Engine), IaaS (AWS EC2); deployed as public, private or hybrid cloud.","IoT connects everyday objects; blockchain is a shared tamper-resistant ledger; Bitcoin (2009) is the first cryptocurrency; AR adds, VR replaces reality.","John McCarthy coined Artificial Intelligence; Alan Turing gave the Turing Test; Ritchie made C, Gosling Java, van Rossum Python, Torvalds Linux.","India: C-DAC (1988), PARAM 8000 (1991), Digital India and DigiLocker (2015), UPI and BHIM (2016), 5G (2022)."]}
);

QB.push(
/* C12 */
["C12","Who is credited with inventing the World Wide Web?",["Tim Berners-Lee","Vint Cerf","Bill Gates","Ray Tomlinson"],"Tim Berners-Lee proposed the WWW at CERN in 1989. Vint Cerf (with Bob Kahn) designed TCP/IP, the internet's protocol – do not mix the web with the internet.","p"],
["C12","The World Wide Web was developed at which organisation?",["CERN","NASA","Bell Labs","IBM"],"Berners-Lee worked at CERN, the European physics laboratory near Geneva, when he created the web (1989–91).","p"],
["C12","ARPANET, the forerunner of today's internet, began in which year?",["1969","1979","1989","1991"],"ARPANET linked its first computers in 1969; 1989–91 is the World Wide Web, a later service on the internet.","p"],
["C12","Which statement about the internet and the WWW is correct?",["The WWW is a service that runs on the internet","The internet is a part of the WWW","Both terms mean exactly the same thing","The WWW is older than the internet"],"The internet is the global network; the web (pages linked by hyperlinks) is one service on it, like e-mail is another.","p"],
["C12","In the URL https://www.ssc.gov.in/login, what is 'https'?",["Protocol","Domain name","Path","Top-level domain"],"The part before :// is the protocol (scheme). ssc.gov.in is the domain and /login is the path.",""],
["C12","What is the main job of DNS?",["Translating domain names into IP addresses","Encrypting web pages","Assigning MAC addresses","Sending e-mail"],"DNS (Domain Name System) works like a phone directory: google.com in, IP address out. Assigning IP addresses is DHCP's job.","p"],
["C12","Which top-level domain is generally used by educational institutions?",[".edu",".org",".com",".net"],".edu is for education, .org for organisations, .com for commercial firms and .net originally for network providers.","p"],
["C12","Which of these is NOT a web browser?",["Google","Mozilla Firefox","Safari","Microsoft Edge"],"Google is a search engine (Google's browser is Chrome). Firefox, Safari and Edge are browsers.","p"],
["C12","Which was the first widely popular graphical web browser, released in 1993?",["Mosaic","Google Chrome","Internet Explorer","Opera"],"NCSA Mosaic (1993) made the web popular; Netscape Navigator followed in 1994, Internet Explorer in 1995 and Chrome in 2008.",""],
["C12","The first or main page of a website is called its:",["Home page","Web portal","Landing server","Bookmark"],"The home page is the starting page of a website. A website is a collection of pages; a web page is a single one.",""],
["C12","Clickable text or an image that takes you to another web page is called a:",["Hyperlink","Hypertext tag","Cookie","Domain"],"Hyperlinks join pages into a web. In HTML they are made with the anchor tag (a).",""],
["C12","HTML documents are saved with which file extension?",[".html or .htm",".hml",".txt only",".web"],".html (or the older 3-letter .htm) is the extension of web pages written in Hyper Text Markup Language.",""],
["C12","Small text files that a website stores in your browser to remember your login and preferences are called:",["Cookies","Cache","Plug-ins","Bookmarks"],"Cookies remember you; cache stores copies of page files (images, scripts) to load pages faster.","p"],
["C12","Saving a web page's address in a browser so you can open it quickly later is called:",["Bookmarking","Caching","Streaming","Uploading"],"A bookmark (Favourite in Edge) stores a link; Ctrl+D adds one in Chrome, Edge and Firefox.",""],
["C12","Sending a file from your computer to a website or server is called:",["Uploading","Downloading","Streaming","Browsing"],"Upload = your device to the internet; download = internet to your device.",""],
["C12","Watching a video online while it is still arriving, without first saving the whole file, is called:",["Streaming","Downloading","Uploading","Surfing"],"Streaming plays media as data arrives; YouTube and online radio stream content.",""],
["C12","A private network inside an organisation that uses internet technology and is open only to its staff is a/an:",["Intranet","Extranet","Internet","Ethernet"],"Intranet = staff only. An extranet lets selected outsiders such as suppliers in. Ethernet is a LAN wiring technology.",""],
["C12","Which company provides you with a connection to the internet?",["Internet Service Provider (ISP)","Search engine","Web browser","Domain registrar"],"ISPs such as BSNL, Jio and Airtel connect you to the internet; a registrar only sells domain names.",""],
["C12","Which top-level domain is the country code for India?",[".in",".ind",".id",".ia"],".in is India's country-code TLD; government sites use .gov.in. (.id belongs to Indonesia.)",""],
["C12","Who founded Google in 1998?",["Larry Page and Sergey Brin","Bill Gates and Paul Allen","Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak","Jerry Yang and David Filo"],"Page and Brin started Google as a Stanford research project. Jerry Yang and David Filo founded Yahoo.",""],
["C12","Which pair is correctly matched?",["Chrome – web browser","Bing – web browser","Firefox – search engine","Safari – search engine"],"Chrome, Firefox and Safari are browsers; Bing (Microsoft) is a search engine.",""],
["C12","Arrange in the correct order of appearance: (1) Google Chrome (2) Mosaic (3) ARPANET (4) World Wide Web",["3, 4, 2, 1","4, 3, 2, 1","3, 2, 4, 1","2, 3, 4, 1"],"ARPANET 1969, WWW 1989–91, Mosaic 1993, Chrome 2008.",""],
/* C13 */
["C13","In e-mail, what does BCC stand for?",["Blind Carbon Copy","Blank Carbon Copy","Basic Carbon Copy","Blind Computer Copy"],"BCC = Blind Carbon Copy. BCC recipients get the mail but their addresses are hidden from all other recipients.","p"],
["C13","Which e-mail field sends a copy to a person without the other recipients knowing about it?",["BCC","CC","To","Subject"],"Only BCC hides the recipient. CC addresses are visible to everyone who receives the mail.",""],
["C13","Who sent the first network e-mail and introduced the @ symbol in addresses?",["Ray Tomlinson","Tim Berners-Lee","Sabeer Bhatia","Vint Cerf"],"Ray Tomlinson sent it on ARPANET in 1971. Sabeer Bhatia co-founded Hotmail (1996).","p"],
["C13","In the address ravi@gmail.com, what is 'gmail.com'?",["Domain name of the mail server","User name","Protocol","Password"],"Before @ is the user name; after @ is the domain where the mailbox is hosted.",""],
["C13","An e-mail that you have written and saved but not yet sent is stored in:",["Drafts","Sent","Outbox","Inbox"],"Drafts hold unfinished, unsent mail. The Outbox holds mail that has been sent off but is waiting to leave, e.g. while offline.","p"],
["C13","Messages waiting to be sent, for example while the computer is offline, are kept in the:",["Outbox","Sent folder","Drafts","Spam"],"Outbox = queued, not yet gone. Once delivered to the server, a copy moves to Sent.",""],
["C13","Unsolicited bulk e-mail is commonly called:",["Spam","Draft","Archive","Cookie"],"Spam (junk) is unwanted bulk mail; mail services filter it into the Spam folder.",""],
["C13","Which option sends your response to the sender and to everyone in the To and CC fields?",["Reply All","Reply","Forward","Compose"],"Reply goes only to the sender; Reply All adds the other To and CC recipients (never hidden BCC ones); Forward sends to new people.","p"],
["C13","Which e-mail action sends a received message, with its attachments, to a new person?",["Forward","Reply","Reply All","Archive"],"Forward includes the original attachments; Reply and Reply All normally do not send them back.",""],
["C13","Text such as name, designation and phone number added automatically at the end of every e-mail is called:",["Signature","Subject","Header","Footer note"],"An e-mail signature is set once in settings and added to each message.",""],
["C13","Which protocol is used to SEND e-mail?",["SMTP","POP3","IMAP","FTP"],"SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) sends; POP3 and IMAP are used to receive mail.","p"],
["C13","What is the maximum size of attachments you can send directly in one Gmail message?",["25 MB","10 MB","50 MB","100 MB"],"Gmail allows 25 MB of attachments; larger files are shared automatically as Google Drive links.",""],
["C13","Hotmail, one of the first free web-mail services (1996), was co-founded by:",["Sabeer Bhatia","Ray Tomlinson","Larry Page","Vint Cerf"],"Sabeer Bhatia and Jack Smith founded Hotmail; Microsoft bought it in 1997.",""],
["C13","NEFT stands for:",["National Electronic Funds Transfer","National Electronic Fund Transaction","Net Electronic Funds Transfer","National E-banking Funds Transfer"],"NEFT is run by RBI and settles transfers in half-hourly batches.","p"],
["C13","RTGS stands for:",["Real Time Gross Settlement","Real Time General Settlement","Rapid Transfer Gross System","Real Transfer Gross Settlement"],"RTGS settles each transfer individually in real time; it is meant for large amounts (minimum Rs 2 lakh).","p"],
["C13","What is the minimum amount that can be sent through RTGS?",["Rs 2 lakh","Rs 1 lakh","Rs 50,000","No minimum"],"RTGS has a Rs 2 lakh minimum and no upper limit. NEFT has no minimum.",""],
["C13","Which organisation developed and operates UPI?",["NPCI","RBI","SBI","SEBI"],"UPI (2016), IMPS, RuPay and BHIM are run by the National Payments Corporation of India. RBI operates NEFT and RTGS.","p"],
["C13","IMPS, the instant 24x7 fund-transfer service, stands for:",["Immediate Payment Service","Instant Money Payment System","Interbank Mobile Payment Service","Immediate Money Processing System"],"IMPS = Immediate Payment Service, launched by NPCI in 2010.",""],
["C13","Which of these transfer systems settles transactions in half-hourly batches rather than one by one?",["NEFT","RTGS","IMPS","UPI"],"NEFT works in batches; RTGS, IMPS and UPI settle each transfer immediately.",""],
["C13","To RECEIVE money through UPI, you need to enter:",["Nothing – no PIN is needed to receive money","Your UPI PIN","Your ATM PIN","An OTP from the payer"],"A UPI PIN is entered only to send money. A request asking for your PIN 'to receive money' is a fraud.",""],
["C13","Logging in with a password and then entering an OTP sent to your phone is an example of:",["Two-factor authentication","Encryption","Phishing","Single sign-on"],"Two different proofs – something you know (password) and something you have (phone/OTP) – make 2FA.",""],
["C13","Which of these is a SAFE digital-banking practice?",["Checking that the bank site starts with https and shows a padlock","Sharing the OTP with a caller from 'the bank'","Writing the ATM PIN on the card","Doing net banking on open public Wi-Fi"],"Banks never ask for PIN, OTP or CVV. Use https sites, keep PINs secret and avoid public Wi-Fi for banking.",""],
/* C14 */
["C14","Which cable is commonly used to connect two computers in a network?",["Ethernet cable","HDMI cable","VGA cable","USB printer cable"],"An Ethernet (twisted-pair, RJ-45) cable links computers; older direct PC-to-PC links used a crossover Ethernet cable. HDMI and VGA carry video.","p"],
["C14","A network that covers a single building, office or school is a:",["LAN","MAN","WAN","PAN"],"LAN = Local Area Network. MAN covers a city, WAN a country or the world, PAN a few metres around one person.","p"],
["C14","The internet is the largest example of a:",["WAN","MAN","LAN","PAN"],"A Wide Area Network spans countries; the internet is the biggest WAN.",""],
["C14","Connecting your mobile phone to wireless earphones through Bluetooth forms a:",["PAN","LAN","MAN","WAN"],"A Personal Area Network covers a few metres around one person.",""],
["C14","Arrange the networks in increasing order of area covered.",["PAN, LAN, MAN, WAN","LAN, PAN, MAN, WAN","PAN, MAN, LAN, WAN","LAN, MAN, PAN, WAN"],"Personal, Local, Metropolitan, Wide – 'Please Let Me Win'.",""],
["C14","In which topology are all computers connected to a central hub or switch?",["Star","Bus","Ring","Mesh"],"Star topology: easy to manage, but if the central device fails the whole network fails.","p"],
["C14","In which topology do all nodes share a single backbone cable?",["Bus","Star","Ring","Tree"],"Bus uses one backbone with terminators at both ends; a break in it stops the network.",""],
["C14","How many cables are needed to connect 6 computers in a full mesh topology?",["15","12","30","36"],"A full mesh needs n(n-1)/2 links: 6 x 5 / 2 = 15.",""],
["C14","Which topology is the most reliable because there are many alternate paths, but needs the most cabling?",["Mesh","Bus","Star","Ring"],"In a mesh every node links to every other node, so one failure does not cut anyone off.",""],
["C14","Which device sends incoming data only to the port of the intended destination?",["Switch","Hub","Repeater","Modem"],"A switch reads MAC addresses and forwards to one port; a hub blindly copies data to all ports.","p"],
["C14","Which device connects different networks and chooses the best path for data packets?",["Router","Hub","Repeater","NIC"],"Routers work at the Network layer using IP addresses. Your home internet box usually contains a modem and a router together.","p"],
["C14","A modem converts:",["Digital signals to analog and analog to digital","Light to electricity only","IP addresses to MAC addresses","Domain names to IP addresses"],"MOdulator-DEModulator: digital computer data is modulated onto analog lines and demodulated back.",""],
["C14","Which device regenerates a weak signal so it can travel further?",["Repeater","Gateway","Switch","Bridge"],"A repeater works at the Physical layer; it simply amplifies/regenerates the signal.",""],
["C14","Which device connects two networks that use DIFFERENT protocols?",["Gateway","Hub","Repeater","Switch"],"A gateway acts as a protocol converter between dissimilar networks.",""],
["C14","Which transmission medium carries data as pulses of light?",["Optical fibre","Coaxial cable","Twisted pair","Microwave"],"Optical fibre works on total internal reflection; it gives the highest bandwidth and is immune to electrical interference.","p"],
["C14","Which is the correct order of the OSI layers from bottom to top?",["Physical, Data link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application","Physical, Network, Data link, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application","Application, Presentation, Session, Transport, Network, Data link, Physical","Physical, Data link, Transport, Network, Presentation, Session, Application"],"Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away. The third option is the correct order but top to bottom.","p"],
["C14","How many layers does the OSI reference model have?",["7","4","5","6"],"OSI (by ISO) has 7 layers; the TCP/IP model has 4.",""],
["C14","Routing of packets between networks using IP addresses is the job of which OSI layer?",["Network layer","Transport layer","Data link layer","Session layer"],"Layer 3 (Network) handles logical addressing and routing; routers work here.",""],
["C14","An IPv6 address is how many bits long?",["128 bits","32 bits","64 bits","48 bits"],"IPv6 = 128 bits; IPv4 = 32 bits; a MAC address = 48 bits.","p"],
["C14","An IPv4 address such as 192.168.1.1 is made up of how many bits?",["32","64","128","16"],"Four octets of 8 bits each = 32 bits, giving about 4.3 billion (2^32) addresses.",""],
["C14","A MAC address, the physical address burnt into a network card, is how long?",["48 bits","32 bits","64 bits","128 bits"],"48 bits = 6 bytes, written as 12 hexadecimal digits, e.g. 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E.",""],
["C14","A walkie-talkie, where both sides can talk but only one at a time, is an example of which mode?",["Half duplex","Simplex","Full duplex","Multiplex"],"Simplex is one way (TV broadcast), half duplex is two ways in turn, full duplex is both at once (telephone).",""],
["C14","The data-carrying capacity of a network channel, measured in bits per second, is called:",["Bandwidth","Latency","Protocol","Topology"],"Bandwidth is expressed in bps, Kbps, Mbps or Gbps.",""],
["C14","In which network model are all computers equal, each able to share and request resources?",["Peer-to-peer","Client-server","Mainframe","Star"],"Peer-to-peer has no central server. In client-server, a central server provides services to clients.",""],
/* C15 */
["C15","What is the full form of HTTP?",["Hyper Text Transfer Protocol","Hyper Text Transport Protocol","High Text Transfer Protocol","Hyper Transfer Text Protocol"],"HTTP = Hyper Text Transfer Protocol, the protocol that carries web pages (port 80). Exams swap Transfer with Transport.","p"],
["C15","What is the full form of HTML?",["Hyper Text Markup Language","Hyper Text Making Language","High Text Markup Language","Hyper Transfer Markup Language"],"HTML = Hyper Text Markup Language – it marks up web pages with tags; it is not a programming language.","p"],
["C15","In computer security, what is the full form of VIRUS?",["Vital Information Resources Under Siege","Very Important Resources Under Siege","Vital Internet Resources Under Security","Virtual Information Resource Utility System"],"VIRUS is popularly expanded as Vital Information Resources Under Siege.","p"],
["C15","URL stands for:",["Uniform Resource Locator","Universal Resource Locator","Uniform Reference Link","Universal Routing Locator"],"URL = Uniform Resource Locator – the address of a resource on the web. 'Universal' is the usual trap.","p"],
["C15","CAPTCHA stands for:",["Completely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart","Computer Automated Public Test to tell Computers and Humans Apart","Completely Automated Program to Tell Computers and Humans Apart","Computerised Automatic Public Turing test to Check Human Access"],"CAPTCHA is a Turing-style test that blocks bots from websites.","p"],
["C15","Which protocol is used to transfer files between computers over the internet?",["FTP","SMTP","HTTP","SNMP"],"FTP (File Transfer Protocol) uses port 21 for control and 20 for data.","p"],
["C15","Which port number does HTTPS use by default?",["443","80","21","25"],"HTTPS uses 443; plain HTTP uses 80; FTP 21; SMTP 25.","p"],
["C15","The default port number for HTTP is:",["80","443","110","53"],"HTTP 80, HTTPS 443, POP3 110, DNS 53.",""],
["C15","Which pair of protocol and port is correctly matched?",["SMTP – 25","DNS – 80","POP3 – 143","SSH – 23"],"DNS is 53, POP3 is 110 (IMAP is 143), SSH is 22 (Telnet is 23).",""],
["C15","Which protocol downloads e-mail from the server to your device, usually removing it from the server?",["POP3","IMAP","SMTP","HTTP"],"POP3 (port 110) downloads; IMAP (port 143) keeps mail on the server, synced across devices.",""],
["C15","Which protocol automatically assigns IP addresses to devices joining a network?",["DHCP","DNS","ARP","FTP"],"DHCP = Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol. DNS resolves names; ARP finds a MAC address from an IP address.","p"],
["C15","Which protocol finds the MAC address that belongs to a known IP address?",["ARP","DHCP","DNS","UDP"],"ARP = Address Resolution Protocol: IP address to MAC address.",""],
["C15","Which statement about TCP and UDP is correct?",["TCP is connection-oriented; UDP is connectionless","TCP is connectionless; UDP is connection-oriented","Both are connectionless","UDP guarantees delivery but TCP does not"],"TCP sets up a connection and guarantees ordered delivery; UDP is faster with no guarantee – used for live video and games.","p"],
["C15","Which protocol provides encrypted remote login to another computer?",["SSH","Telnet","FTP","SMTP"],"SSH (Secure Shell, port 22) encrypts; Telnet (port 23) sends everything, even passwords, as plain text.",""],
["C15","VoIP, used for internet voice calls, stands for:",["Voice over Internet Protocol","Voice of Internet Phone","Video over IP","Voice over Intranet Path"],"VoIP carries voice calls as data packets over IP networks.",""],
["C15","What does VPN stand for?",["Virtual Private Network","Virtual Public Network","Very Private Network","Visual Private Network"],"A VPN creates an encrypted tunnel over a public network such as the internet.",""],
["C15","GUI stands for:",["Graphical User Interface","Graphical Unified Interface","General User Interface","Graphic Utility Interface"],"GUI uses windows, icons and a mouse; the alternative is the CLI (Command Line Interface).",""],
["C15","The image format JPEG stands for:",["Joint Photographic Experts Group","Joint Picture Experts Group","Joint Photographic Electronic Graphics","Java Photographic Experts Group"],"JPEG is a compressed photo format; GIF = Graphics Interchange Format; PNG = Portable Network Graphics.",""],
["C15","Which full form is INCORRECT?",["GIF – Graphical Image File","PNG – Portable Network Graphics","MPEG – Moving Picture Experts Group","GPS – Global Positioning System"],"GIF actually stands for Graphics Interchange Format.",""],
["C15","CERT-In stands for:",["Indian Computer Emergency Response Team","Indian Cyber Emergency Recovery Team","Central Emergency Response Team – India","Computer Emergency Registration Team – India"],"CERT-In is India's national agency for responding to cyber-security incidents, under MeitY.",""],
["C15","IoT stands for:",["Internet of Things","Internet of Technology","Integration of Things","Intranet of Things"],"IoT connects everyday objects with sensors to the internet.",""],
["C15","Which protocol converts a domain name such as india.gov.in into its IP address?",["DNS","DHCP","SMTP","ARP"],"DNS (Domain Name System, port 53) is the internet's directory service.",""],
/* C16 */
["C16","Which malware copies itself across a network on its own, without needing to attach to a host file?",["Worm","Virus","Trojan horse","Adware"],"A worm self-replicates through networks; a virus needs a host file and a user to open it.","p"],
["C16","Malicious software that looks like a useful program but secretly harms the system is a:",["Trojan horse","Worm","Firewall","Cookie"],"A Trojan relies on disguise and does not replicate itself – named after the wooden horse of Troy.","p"],
["C16","Malware that encrypts your files and demands payment to unlock them is called:",["Ransomware","Spyware","Adware","Rootkit"],"Ransomware holds data hostage; WannaCry (2017) was a famous worldwide attack. Backups are the best defence.","p"],
["C16","Software that secretly records every key you press to steal passwords is a:",["Keylogger","Adware","Firewall","Worm"],"Keyloggers capture keystrokes; on-screen keyboards on banking sites help defeat them.",""],
["C16","Software that hides deep inside the system to give an attacker hidden administrator-level control is a:",["Rootkit","Adware","Cookie","Patch"],"Rootkits conceal themselves and other malware, which makes them hard to detect.",""],
["C16","A network of infected computers controlled remotely by an attacker is called a:",["Botnet","Firewall","Intranet","Honeypot"],"Each infected machine is a bot or zombie; botnets are used for spam and DDoS attacks.",""],
["C16","An e-mail pretending to be from your bank and asking you to click a link and enter your password is an example of:",["Phishing","Spamming only","Hacking by brute force","Pharming"],"Phishing uses fake messages to steal credentials. Pharming redirects you to a fake site without your clicking a link.","p"],
["C16","A fraud carried out through phone calls in which the caller pretends to be a bank official is called:",["Vishing","Smishing","Pharming","Spoofing"],"Vishing = voice phishing; smishing = SMS phishing.","p"],
["C16","Phishing done through SMS text messages is called:",["Smishing","Vishing","Whaling","Pharming"],"SMS + phishing = smishing.",""],
["C16","Redirecting users to a fake website even when they type the correct web address, for example by tampering with DNS, is called:",["Pharming","Phishing","Spamming","Sniffing"],"Pharming poisons the path to the site; phishing tricks you into clicking a fake link.",""],
["C16","Flooding a website with traffic from thousands of infected computers so that genuine users cannot reach it is a:",["DDoS attack","Phishing attack","SQL injection","Keylogging"],"DDoS = Distributed Denial of Service, usually launched from a botnet.","p"],
["C16","Typing malicious database commands into a website's login or search box is called:",["SQL injection","Brute force attack","Spoofing","Smishing"],"SQL injection attacks a website's database through its input fields.",""],
["C16","Trying every possible combination of characters until the correct password is found is a:",["Brute force attack","Man-in-the-middle attack","Phishing attack","Pharming attack"],"Long, complex passwords and account lock-outs defeat brute force.",""],
["C16","Which security system monitors and filters incoming and outgoing network traffic according to set rules?",["Firewall","Antivirus","Encryption","Backup"],"A firewall guards the network gate; antivirus finds and removes malware already on the system.","p"],
["C16","Converting readable data (plaintext) into an unreadable form (ciphertext) is called:",["Encryption","Decryption","Compression","Formatting"],"Encryption locks data with a key; decryption turns ciphertext back into plaintext.","p"],
["C16","An ethical hacker who tests systems with the owner's permission to find weaknesses is a:",["White hat hacker","Black hat hacker","Grey hat hacker","Script kiddie"],"White hat = ethical; black hat = criminal; grey hat = breaks in without permission but without malicious intent.",""],
["C16","Which of these is NOT a good password practice?",["Using your date of birth as the password","Using a long mix of letters, numbers and symbols","Using different passwords for different accounts","Enabling two-factor authentication"],"Personal details such as birth dates are easy to guess.",""],
["C16","Which of these is NOT a type of malware?",["Firewall","Spyware","Adware","Worm"],"A firewall is a security tool; spyware, adware and worms are malicious software.",""],
["C16","The padlock icon and 'https' in a browser's address bar mainly show that:",["The connection to the site is encrypted","The site is free of all viruses","The site is owned by the government","The page has been bookmarked"],"HTTPS uses SSL/TLS encryption; it does not by itself prove a site is honest.",""],
["C16","A digital signature is used to:",["Verify the sender and show that the document was not altered","Compress a document","Hide a document from search engines","Scan a document for viruses"],"The sender signs with a private key; anyone can verify with the public key. The IT Act 2000 gave digital signatures legal validity.",""],
["C16","India's main law dealing with cyber crime and electronic records is the:",["Information Technology Act, 2000","Cyber Security Act, 2005","Digital India Act, 2015","Telegraph Act, 1885"],"The IT Act 2000 was amended in 2008, adding offences such as cyber terrorism (Section 66F).","p"],
["C16","What is the national helpline number for reporting cyber financial fraud in India?",["1930","100","1098","112"],"Dial 1930 or report on cybercrime.gov.in. 1098 is Childline; 112 is the all-emergency number.",""],
["C16","Which was the first virus for MS-DOS PCs, created in 1986?",["Brain","Creeper","ILOVEYOU","WannaCry"],"Brain (1986, Pakistan) was the first PC virus; ILOVEYOU was a 2000 worm and WannaCry 2017 ransomware.",""],
/* C17 */
["C17","Who is known as the father of Artificial Intelligence for coining the term 'Artificial Intelligence'?",["John McCarthy","Alan Turing","Charles Babbage","Marvin Minsky"],"John McCarthy coined the term for the 1956 Dartmouth workshop. Alan Turing laid the theory and proposed the Turing Test (1950).","p"],
["C17","The test that checks whether a machine can show behaviour indistinguishable from a human's was proposed by:",["Alan Turing","John McCarthy","Ada Lovelace","Tim Berners-Lee"],"The Turing Test was proposed by Alan Turing in 1950.","p"],
["C17","Who is regarded as the first computer programmer?",["Ada Lovelace","Charles Babbage","Grace Hopper","Alan Turing"],"Ada Lovelace wrote an algorithm for Babbage's Analytical Engine; Babbage is the father of the computer.",""],
["C17","Which statement about AI, machine learning and deep learning is correct?",["Deep learning is a subset of machine learning, which is a subset of AI","AI is a subset of machine learning","Machine learning is a subset of deep learning","All three are unrelated fields"],"AI is the widest field; ML learns from data; deep learning is ML with many-layered neural networks.",""],
["C17","Using Gmail or Google Docs through a web browser is an example of which cloud service model?",["SaaS","PaaS","IaaS","DaaS"],"Software as a Service gives ready-to-use software online. PaaS gives a platform to build apps; IaaS gives virtual hardware.","p"],
["C17","Renting virtual servers, storage and networking from a cloud provider is:",["IaaS","SaaS","PaaS","P2P"],"Infrastructure as a Service – e.g. Amazon EC2. You manage the software yourself.",""],
["C17","A cloud used by only one organisation is called a:",["Private cloud","Public cloud","Community network","Hybrid cloud"],"Public clouds are shared; hybrid mixes public and private.",""],
["C17","Everyday objects such as smart meters and watches connected to the internet form the:",["Internet of Things","World Wide Web","Intranet","Big Data"],"IoT devices have sensors and send data over the internet.","p"],
["C17","A distributed, tamper-resistant digital ledger shared across many computers is called:",["Blockchain","Cloud storage","Database server","Cache"],"Blockchain records cannot easily be changed because many copies exist; Bitcoin is built on it.",""],
["C17","Which technology adds digital objects to your view of the real world, as in Pokemon Go?",["Augmented Reality","Virtual Reality","Quantum computing","Edge computing"],"AR adds to reality; VR replaces it with a fully computer-made world seen through a headset.",""],
["C17","The basic unit of information in a quantum computer is the:",["Qubit","Bit only","Byte","Nibble"],"A qubit can be in a superposition of 0 and 1 at the same time.",""],
["C17","Processing data close to where it is generated instead of in a distant data centre is called:",["Edge computing","Cloud computing","Grid computing","Mainframe computing"],"Edge computing reduces delay, e.g. for sensors in self-driving cars.",""],
["C17","Who created the Python programming language?",["Guido van Rossum","James Gosling","Dennis Ritchie","Bjarne Stroustrup"],"Python appeared in 1991. Gosling made Java, Ritchie made C, Stroustrup made C++.","p"],
["C17","James Gosling is known as the father of which programming language?",["Java","C","Python","JavaScript"],"Gosling created Java at Sun Microsystems (released 1995). JavaScript is a different language.","p"],
["C17","Who developed the C programming language at Bell Labs?",["Dennis Ritchie","Ken Thompson alone","Linus Torvalds","James Gosling"],"Dennis Ritchie created C (early 1970s) and co-created UNIX with Ken Thompson.","p"],
["C17","The Linux kernel was created in 1991 by:",["Linus Torvalds","Richard Stallman","Bill Gates","Steve Wozniak"],"Linux is open source; Torvalds released it while a student in Finland.",""],
["C17","Which pair of company and founders is correctly matched?",["Microsoft – Bill Gates and Paul Allen","Apple – Larry Page and Sergey Brin","Google – Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak","Facebook – Bill Gates"],"Microsoft (1975) – Gates and Allen; Apple (1976) – Jobs and Wozniak; Google (1998) – Page and Brin; Facebook (2004) – Mark Zuckerberg.",""],
["C17","India's first supercomputer, PARAM 8000, was developed by:",["C-DAC","ISRO","DRDO","BARC"],"C-DAC (Centre for Development of Advanced Computing, Pune) built PARAM 8000 in 1991 under Vijay Bhatkar.","p"],
["C17","The Digital India programme was launched in which year?",["2015","2010","2016","2019"],"Digital India was launched on 1 July 2015; DigiLocker was launched the same year.",""],
["C17","DigiLocker is mainly used to:",["Store and share official documents digitally","Make UPI payments","File income-tax returns only","Book railway tickets"],"DigiLocker is a government cloud locker for documents such as driving licences and marksheets.",""],
["C17","Aadhaar numbers are issued by which body?",["UIDAI","NPCI","C-DAC","CERT-In"],"The Unique Identification Authority of India issues the 12-digit Aadhaar number.",""],
["C17","The first cryptocurrency, launched in 2009, is:",["Bitcoin","Ethereum","Dogecoin","Litecoin"],"Bitcoin was created by the pseudonymous Satoshi Nakamoto and runs on a blockchain.",""],
["C17","Big Data is commonly described by which three Vs?",["Volume, Velocity, Variety","Value, Vision, Volume","Velocity, Virtual, Version","Variety, Vector, Validity"],"Volume (how much), Velocity (how fast) and Variety (how many kinds of data).",""]
);

